sociated thermodynamic quantities through its derivatives. The convexity properties of the system are examined in the configuration space {\\{1,2,3\\}}^{{V}_{n}}, providing further insights into the structure of the Gibbs measures. Finally, to study the dynamical features of the model — such as chaoticity, periodicity, and bifurcations — we calculate the Lyapunov exponent, offering a deeper understanding of the system’s complex behavior. Furthermore, the dynamical evolution toward the thermodynamic limit is characterized through a 2D stability analysis based on the Oseledets theorem. By employing trajectory embedding, we identify global basin restructuring at the phase transition thresholds and confirm the structural stability of the model, rigorously proving the absence of chaotic attractors within the physical parameter space.\n\n## Introduction\n\nStudies of the q-state Potts model have revealed rich critical behaviors and complex phase diagrams, particularly in systems with competing interactions or geometric constraints. Qin et al. [1] demonstrated that antiferromagnetic Potts models can display phases with partial long-range order, where spatial correlations emerge only among specific subsets of spin variables. This phenomenon results from the intricate interplay between the discrete symmetry of the Potts spins and the frustration imposed by the lattice geometry or competing interactions. Gibbs measures associated with the q-state Potts models are fundamental in statistical mechanics, mathematical biology, and the numerous applications, describing equilibrium states of interacting particle systems [2], [3], [4], [5]. A central problem in statistical mechanics is the theoretical investigation of phase transitions in lattice-based systems. For lattice spin models, the existence of multiple Gibbs measures is a key indicator of phase transitions [6], [7].\n\nVarious methods have been developed in the literature to establish sufficient conditions for extremality, often by reducing the analysis to a finite-dimensional optimization problem that depends solely on the transition (or stochastic) matrix. These include the symmetric entropy method introduced in [27], percolation-based techniques presented in [28], [29], and the accessible bound formulated by Martin for the binary asymmetric channel [30]. Building upon the algebraic structure of the models, the approach of [28] was modified by [31] to provide explicit constraints on the transition parameters. The concept of tree-indexed Markov chains, originally formulated in [29], was subsequently extended to lattice models on CTs of arbitrary order by [31]. In more recent developments, [20], [23] examined TIGMs for the Potts SOS model—a generalization of both the Potts and SOS models—focusing on the characterization of these measures as either extremal or nonextremal within the broader space of Gibbs measures (cf. [32], [33], [34]). By interpreting distinct branches of the CT as physically separate interaction channels, the asymmetric coupling constants acquire a clear physical interpretation: they quantify directional bias in information propagation, magnetization transport, or correlation spreading. Thus, the model represents not merely a mathematical extension of the classical Potts framework, but rather a physically motivated approach for investigating realistic non-uniform systems. Moreover, this framework permits a detailed analysis of dynamical phase transitions in the asymmetric Potts model under consideration, by examining the complex dynamics of the associated renormalization group maps [42].\n\nAcross these experimental platforms [43], [44], [45], [46], the key common feature is the ability to program an interaction graph and tune effective couplings during controlled (near-)adiabatic state preparation. In neutral-atom implementations, interaction strengths can be engineered through geometry, detuning, and dressing protocols, while in superconducting and trapped-ion optimizers the effective couplings and constraints are controlled by programmable couplers and annealing schedules. ## Preliminaries and definitions\n\nIn this section, we briefly introduce the basic definitions and notations used throughout this paper.\n\n## Construction of Gibbs measures and phase transition phenomenon\n\nBy standard techniques, for a given configuration {\\sigma }_{n}\\in {\\Omega }^{{V}_{n}}, we construct the finite-volume Gibbs measure for the system as {\\mu }_{n}^{h}\\left({\\sigma }_{n}\\right)=\\frac{1}{{Z}_{n}}{e}^{\\left(-\\beta {H}_{n}\\left({\\sigma }_{n}\\right)+\\sum _{x\\in {W}_{n}}{h}_{\\sigma \\left(x\\right),x}\\right)},where {Z}_{n} is the partition function ensuring normalization, \\beta is the inverse temperature, {H}_{n}\\left(\\sigma \\right) is the Hamiltonian of the system, h represents an external magnetic field, and {V}_{n} denotes the set of lattice vertices at scale n.\n\nThe partition function {Z}_{n} for the finite-volume Gibbs measure {\\mu }^{\\left(n\\right)} is defined as {Z}_{n}=\\sum _{\\sigma \\in {\\Omega }^{{V}_{n}}}exp\\left(-\\beta {H}_{n}\\left(\\sigma \\right)+\\sum _{x\\in {W}_{n}}\\sum _{s=1}^{3}{h}_{\\sigma \\left(x\\right),x}\\right),\n## Lyapunov exponent and trajectory stability analysis\n\nThe Lyapunov exponent serves as a crucial quantitative measure for characterizing chaotic behavior in nonlinear dynamical systems [54], [55]. Its application has proven particularly valuable in the analysis of single-variable systems, including logical mappings and discrete transformations [41], [56]. Recent investigations have extended this analysis to lattice models on the CTs, where the Lyapunov exponent has been instrumental in identifying chaotic regimes, bifurcation points, and periodic\n\n## Conclusions\n\nIn this work, we have conducted a systematic investigation of the Gibbs measures for a three-state asymmetric Potts model on a second-order Cayley tree subject to an external field. The core of our analysis 